steps.body:
Real input often packs several values into one line, like `alice,90,85,77`. Read the whole line with `std::getline(std::cin, line)` as in the last lesson, then wrap it in a `std::istringstream` from `<sstream>`. That turns the string into a stream you can read from, just like `std::cin`.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::string line = "alice,90,85,77";
    {{code}}
    std::cout << "name: " << name << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::istringstream iss(line);
std::string name;
std::getline(iss, name, ',');  // reads up to the next comma
```

The third argument to `std::getline` is the delimiter. It reads up to that character and drops it. Like `std::getline(std::cin, line)`, it returns the stream, which turns `false` once nothing is left to read. So the first call takes the label and a loop takes the rest:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::string line = "alice,90,85,77";
    std::istringstream iss(line);
    std::string name;
    std::getline(iss, name, ',');
    int total = 0;
    {{code}}
    std::cout << name << ' ' << total << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::string part;
while (std::getline(iss, part, ',')) {
    total += std::stoi(part);
}
```

Every part is a `std::string`, so convert numbers with `std::stoi` (or `std::stod` for decimals). `std::stoi` skips leading spaces, but an empty part such as the one in `a,,3` makes it throw an error that stops the program. Assume the input matches the format.

When the values are separated by spaces instead, you do not need a delimiter: `iss >> token` reads one word at a time, exactly like `std::cin >> token`. The stream keeps its own copy of the line, so changing `line` later does not affect it.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::string line;
    while (std::getline(std::cin, line)) {
        std::istringstream iss(line);
        std::string name;
        std::getline(iss, name, ',');  // the label before the first comma
        int total = 0;
        int count = 0;
        std::string part;
        while (std::getline(iss, part, ',')) {
            total += std::stoi(part);
            ++count;
        }
        std::cout << name << ' ' << total << " (" << count
                  << (count == 1 ? " score)" : " scores)") << '\n';
    }
    return 0;
}

