steps.body:
`++counts[word]` counts anything while you read it. A missing key is inserted with 0 and then incremented, so you never check first.

```stdin
red blue green blue red
```

```cpp
std::map<std::string, int> counts;
std::string word;
while (std::cin >> word) {
    ++counts[word];
}
for (const auto& [w, n] : counts) {
    std::cout << w << '=' << n << ' ';  // blue=2 green=1 red=2
}
```

A `std::map` keeps its keys sorted, so a range-for prints the counts in alphabetical order with no extra sorting. Each map node lives on the heap, and the map owns them all.

To find the most frequent key, walk the map once and replace the best only when a count is **strictly greater**:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::map<std::string, int> counts = {{"blue", 2}, {"green", 1}, {"red", 2}};
    std::string best;
    int bestCount = 0;
    for (const auto& [w, count] : counts) {
        {{code}}
    }
    std::cout << "most: " << best << " (" << bestCount << ")\n";
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
if (count > bestCount) {
    best = w;
    bestCount = count;
}
```

Because the map visits keys in order, the first key with the top count wins, and a later key with the same count does not replace it. Ties go to the alphabetically first key with no extra code. Using `>=` would hand ties to the last key instead.

> Gotcha: `>>` keeps case and punctuation, so `The`, `the`, and `the.` are three different keys.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <string>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> counts;
    std::string word;
    while (std::cin >> word) {
        ++counts[word];  // a new word starts at 0, then becomes 1
    }

    std::string best;
    int bestCount = 0;
    for (const auto& [w, count] : counts) {
        std::cout << w << ' ' << count << '\n';
        if (count > bestCount) {  // strictly greater: ties keep the earlier word
            best = w;
            bestCount = count;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "most: " << best << " (" << bestCount << ")\n";
    return 0;
}

