steps.body:
Many problems give you a list and then questions about it. The input comes in two sections: a count `n` and `n` items, then a count `q` and `q` queries. Read each section with its own counted loop.

Load the items into a `std::set`. `insert` ignores duplicates, and the set stays sorted, so one container removes repeats and orders the data:

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    {{code}}
    for (int x : numbers) {
        std::cout << x << ' ';
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

```stdin
6
5 3 9 3 5 1
```

```cpp
std::set<int> numbers;
for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
    int x = 0;
    std::cin >> x;
    numbers.insert(x);
}
```

Then answer each query with `count`, which returns 1 when the value is in the set and 0 when it is not. Each lookup is O(log n) because a set is a balanced tree, so thousands of queries stay fast. A `std::vector` with `std::find` would scan every item for every query.

```cpp harness
#include <bits/stdc++.h>

int main() {
    const std::set<int> numbers = {1, 3, 5, 9};
    const std::vector<int> queries = {3, 4, 9};
    for (int query : queries) {
        {{code}}
    }
    return 0;
}
```

```cpp
std::cout << (numbers.count(query) == 1 ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';
```

Wrap the ternary in parentheses inside `std::cout`. A range-for over the set prints the distinct values in ascending order.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <set>

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    std::cin >> n;
    std::set<int> numbers;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        int x = 0;
        std::cin >> x;
        numbers.insert(x);  // duplicates are ignored
    }

    int q = 0;
    std::cin >> q;
    for (int i = 0; i < q; ++i) {
        int query = 0;
        std::cin >> query;
        std::cout << (numbers.count(query) == 1 ? "yes" : "no") << '\n';
    }

    bool first = true;
    for (int x : numbers) {
        if (!first) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << x;
        first = false;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}

